    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find \\(\\det(\\mathbf{M}^4)\\) given that \\(\\det(\\mathbf{M}) = -2\\), we can use the properties of determinants. Specifically, we use the property that states the determinant of a matrix raised to a power is the determinant of the matrix raised to that power. This property can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{M}^n) = (\\det(\\mathbf{M}))^n\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, we are given that \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is a \\(2 \\times 2\\) matrix, so \\(n = 4\\). Therefore, we can substitute \\(\\det(\\mathbf{M}) = -2\\) into the property:\n\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{M}^4) = (\\det(\\mathbf{M}))^4 = (-2)^4\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\((-2)^4\\):\n\n\\[\n(-2)^4 = (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2) = 16\n\\]\n\nThus, the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
