    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given that \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2\\), we need to find \\(\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4)\\).\n\nFirst, recall the property of determinants for squaring a matrix. For any square matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}^2\\) is the square of the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}\\). That is,\n\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^2) = (\\det \\mathbf{M})^2\n\\]\n\nApplying this property to \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (\\det \\mathbf{M}^2)^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = \\left( (\\det \\mathbf{M})^2 \\right)^2 = \\left( (-2)^2 \\right)^2\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n(-2)^2 = 4\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n\\left( (-2)^2 \\right)^2 = 4^2 = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
